---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ChessInterface from '../components/ChessInterface';
import { STORIES } from '../data/stories';
---

<Layout title="ChessNarrate — Strategic Analysis">
	<section class="max-w-7xl w-full mx-auto flex flex-col gap-6">
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-2 max-w-2xl">
			<h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-sans font-semibold tracking-tight text-brand-ink">
				Analyze your chess games.
			</h1>
			<p class="text-brand-body text-sm md:text-base leading-relaxed">
				Upload a FEN string or paste a full PGN game to step through the moves. Client-side engines do the calculations while the Narrator analyzes the story.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="mt-4">
			<ChessInterface client:only="react" />
		</div>

		<!-- Interactive Chess Stories Section -->
		<section class="mt-12 border-t border-brand-hairline pt-12 flex flex-col gap-6 font-sans select-none">
			<div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
				<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-brand-ink">
					Learn from Chess History<span class="text-brand-accent">.</span>
				</h2>
				<p class="text-brand-body text-sm leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
					Step through legendary games with AI commentary and test your skills with interactive guess-the-move challenges.
				</p>
			</div>

			<!-- Stories Grid -->
			<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-5 mt-2">
				{STORIES.slice(0, 3).map((story) => (
					<a 
						href={`/stories/${story.slug}`} 
						class="group flex flex-col gap-3 p-5 rounded-md border border-brand-hairline bg-brand-canvas hover:bg-brand-canvas-soft2 hover:border-brand-accent/30 transition-all duration-200 shadow-lvl1 hover:shadow-lvl3 cursor-pointer"
					>
						<div class="flex items-center justify-between">
							<span class="text-2xl">{story.badge.icon}</span>
							<span class="text-[10px] font-mono text-brand-mute border border-brand-hairline rounded-full px-2 py-0.5">{story.year}</span>
						</div>
						<h3 class="text-base font-semibold tracking-tight text-brand-ink group-hover:text-brand-accent transition-colors">{story.title}</h3>
						<p class="text-xs text-brand-body leading-relaxed line-clamp-2 flex-grow">{story.description}</p>
						<span class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-accent mt-2 group-hover:translate-x-0.5 transition-transform">Play Story &rarr;</span>
					</a>
				))}
			</div>
			<a href="/stories" class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-accent hover:underline flex items-center gap-1.5 w-fit mt-2">
				View All Stories &rarr;
			</a>
		</section>

		<!-- SEO Optimized Article: Chess Analysis -->
		<article class="mt-16 border-t border-brand-hairline pt-12 max-w-4xl flex flex-col gap-6 font-sans select-none">
			<header class="flex flex-col gap-2">
				<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-brand-ink">
					Deep-Dive Chess Analysis with Stockfish & AI Commentary
				</h2>
				<p class="text-xs font-mono text-brand-mute uppercase tracking-wider">
					Optimize Your Strategy & Decode Your Position
				</p>
			</header>

			<div class="text-brand-body text-sm leading-relaxed flex flex-col gap-4">
				<p>
					Welcome to the ultimate web platform for modern, interactive <strong>chess analysis</strong>. Whether you are an aspiring amateur building your baseline knowledge or a seasoned tournament competitor preparing opening preparation, improving at chess requires a deep understanding of your mistakes. Traditional chess engines provide raw mathematical evaluations (such as +1.5 or -2.4), but they fail to explain the <em>positional logic</em> behind their calculations. Our platform bridges this gap by combining a powerful client-side <strong>chess analysis engine</strong> with an intelligent, voice-synthesized AI Narrator. We provide a completely <strong>free chess analysis</strong> environment designed to help you decode the complexities of the board, analyze move quality, and accelerate your learning path.
				</p>

				<h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-brand-ink mt-4 uppercase font-mono tracking-wide border-b border-brand-hairline pb-1.5">
					The Power of Interactive Chess Analysis Boards
				</h3>
				<p>
					Using our responsive <strong>chess analysis board</strong>, you can explore lines, test hypothetical variations, and analyze recent online matches. With full support for <strong>pgn chess analysis</strong>, you can copy and paste complete game records directly into the tool. The engine will instantly parse the game history, populate the interactive board, build a momentum line chart, and step through every move with precise evaluations.
				</p>
				<p>
					This <strong>online chess analysis</strong> portal runs entirely in your local web browser. There is no software to download, no sign-ups required, and no hidden subscriptions. You get access to premium features of a professional-grade <strong>chess analysis free</strong> tool, leveraging Stockfish directly on your device. As you step through your games, the line chart visualizes the swing of evaluation, showing exactly where you held the upper hand and where the game slipped away.
				</p>

				<h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-brand-ink mt-4 uppercase font-mono tracking-wide border-b border-brand-hairline pb-1.5">
					Play Chess Against Computer with Real-Time Stockfish Analysis
				</h3>
				<p>
					Looking to test your skills in real time? Switch to our play mode and <strong>play chess against computer</strong> opponents. Backed by client-side <strong>stockfish analysis</strong>, you can configure the engine's skill level to match your expertise, ranging from Easy and Medium up to Hard and Master. 
				</p>
				<p>
					Playing against a computer is one of the most effective ways to build board vision and tactical discipline. Instead of silent calculation, our AI Narrator talks back to you after every turn. If you make a mistake, the narrator will point out what you missed, giving you immediate tactical feedback. If you deliver a brilliant checkmate threat, the narrator will mock the computer's impending doom. This feedback loop makes playing against the computer an engaging, educational experience.
				</p>

				<h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-brand-ink mt-4 uppercase font-mono tracking-wide border-b border-brand-hairline pb-1.5">
					Learn Chess Openings and Build Positional Mastery
				</h3>
				<p>
					Every great chess player starts by understanding the first phase of the game. Our analysis board recognizes standard opening theory and helps you <strong>learn chess openings</strong> by identifying them as they appear on the board (such as the Fried Liver Attack, Queen's Gambit, or Ruy Lopez). By studying opening theory with real-time engine evaluations, you can understand <em>why</em> certain moves are book recommendations and <em>how</em> minor deviations can compromise your pawn structure or king safety.
				</p>

				<h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-brand-ink mt-4 uppercase font-mono tracking-wide border-b border-brand-hairline pb-1.5">
					Move Quality Classifications
				</h3>
				<p>
					To help you categorize your play, our system uses a comprehensive classification system:
				</p>
				<ul class="list-disc list-inside flex flex-col gap-1.5 pl-2 text-xs font-mono text-brand-body">
					<li><strong class="text-[#26c6da]">Brilliant (!!)</strong>: Exceptional, unexpected moves that turn the game in your favor.</li>
					<li><strong class="text-emerald-500">Best Move (!)</strong>: The top recommendation of the engine that maintains your advantage.</li>
					<li><strong class="text-[#66bb6a]">Excellent (★)</strong>: Solid alternatives that keep you in a highly favorable position.</li>
					<li><strong class="text-emerald-600">Good Move (!)</strong>: Sound moves that maintain dynamic equality or a slight edge.</li>
					<li><strong class="text-slate-400">Only Move (□)</strong>: The single legal or playable response to escape danger.</li>
					<li><strong class="text-[#78909c]">Unclear (∞)</strong>: Complex positions where neither side has a decisive advantage.</li>
				</ul>
				<p class="mt-2 text-xs">
					By reviewing your game statistics, you can pinpoint the frequency of your blunders (??), mistakes (?), and dubious moves (?!), giving you a clear roadmap of what tactical areas to study next. Start analyzing today!
				</p>
			</div>
		</article>

		<!-- FAQ Section -->
		<section class="mt-16 border-t border-brand-hairline pt-12 max-w-4xl flex flex-col gap-6 font-sans">
			<header class="flex flex-col gap-2">
				<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-brand-ink">
					Frequently Asked Questions
				</h2>
				<p class="text-xs font-mono text-brand-mute uppercase tracking-wider">
					Learn More About Chess Analysis, Engine Evaluators, and ChessNarrate
				</p>
			</header>

			<div class="flex flex-col gap-4 mt-2 select-none">
				<details class="group border border-brand-hairline rounded-md bg-brand-canvas p-4 transition-all duration-300 hover:border-brand-hairline-strong shadow-lvl2 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
					<summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-sans font-semibold text-sm text-brand-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
						<span>What do the numbers mean in chess analysis?</span>
						<span class="transition-transform duration-300 group-open:rotate-180 text-brand-mute">
							<svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2.5" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path></svg>
						</span>
					</summary>
					<div class="mt-3 text-brand-body text-sm font-sans leading-relaxed border-t border-brand-hairline pt-3">
						In chess analysis, the numbers represent the evaluation score, measured in centipawns (where 100 centipawns equal 1 pawn value). A positive score (e.g., +1.50) indicates an advantage for White, while a negative score (e.g., -2.30) indicates an advantage for Black. If the score reads mate threats (like M4), it means forced checkmate is available in 4 moves.
					</div>
				</details>

				<details class="group border border-brand-hairline rounded-md bg-brand-canvas p-4 transition-all duration-300 hover:border-brand-hairline-strong shadow-lvl2 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
					<summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-sans font-semibold text-sm text-brand-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
						<span>How to read chess analysis?</span>
						<span class="transition-transform duration-300 group-open:rotate-180 text-brand-mute">
							<svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2.5" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path></svg>
						</span>
					</summary>
					<div class="mt-3 text-brand-body text-sm font-sans leading-relaxed border-t border-brand-hairline pt-3">
						Reading chess analysis involves interpreting evaluation graphs and move qualities. Look at the evaluation bar next to the board to see who has the advantage. Check the move ratings (like Best Move, Excellent, or Blunder) to see how your moves compared to the engine's top choices. The evaluation line chart shows the flow of momentum over time, helping you identify critical turning points in the game.
					</div>
				</details>

				<details class="group border border-brand-hairline rounded-md bg-brand-canvas p-4 transition-all duration-300 hover:border-brand-hairline-strong shadow-lvl2 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
					<summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-sans font-semibold text-sm text-brand-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
						<span>What is game rating in Chess.com analysis?</span>
						<span class="transition-transform duration-300 group-open:rotate-180 text-brand-mute">
							<svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2.5" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path></svg>
						</span>
					</summary>
					<div class="mt-3 text-brand-body text-sm font-sans leading-relaxed border-t border-brand-hairline pt-3">
						In Chess.com analysis, the game rating (or accuracy percentage) is a score from 0 to 100 that measures how closely your moves matched the engine's top choices. It calculates this using a proprietary formula that correlates move quality classifications with expected performance. A higher rating indicates fewer blunders and a more accurate game.
					</div>
				</details>

				<details class="group border border-brand-hairline rounded-md bg-brand-canvas p-4 transition-all duration-300 hover:border-brand-hairline-strong shadow-lvl2 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
					<summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-sans font-semibold text-sm text-brand-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
						<span>How accurate is ChessNarrate compared to Chess.com or Lichess?</span>
						<span class="transition-transform duration-300 group-open:rotate-180 text-brand-mute">
							<svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2.5" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path></svg>
						</span>
					</summary>
					<div class="mt-3 text-brand-body text-sm font-sans leading-relaxed border-t border-brand-hairline pt-3">
						ChessNarrate runs the official Stockfish chess analysis engine directly in your browser, providing the same professional-grade evaluation accuracy as Chess.com or Lichess. The key difference is our AI Narrator, which translates those cold engine evaluation numbers into natural language critiques, helping you understand the strategic concepts behind the moves.
					</div>
				</details>

				<details class="group border border-brand-hairline rounded-md bg-brand-canvas p-4 transition-all duration-300 hover:border-brand-hairline-strong shadow-lvl2 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
					<summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-sans font-semibold text-sm text-brand-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
						<span>Can I upload games from Chess.com or Lichess?</span>
						<span class="transition-transform duration-300 group-open:rotate-180 text-brand-mute">
							<svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2.5" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path></svg>
						</span>
					</summary>
					<div class="mt-3 text-brand-body text-sm font-sans leading-relaxed border-t border-brand-hairline pt-3">
						Yes! You can easily export any game from Chess.com or Lichess as a standard PGN (Portable Game Notation) file or copy its FEN (Forsyth-Edwards Notation) string. Paste the PGN or FEN text directly into our input box to run a complete analysis.
					</div>
				</details>

				<details class="group border border-brand-hairline rounded-md bg-brand-canvas p-4 transition-all duration-300 hover:border-brand-hairline-strong shadow-lvl2 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
					<summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-sans font-semibold text-sm text-brand-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
						<span>What makes a chess move a blunder vs a mistake?</span>
						<span class="transition-transform duration-300 group-open:rotate-180 text-brand-mute">
							<svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2.5" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path></svg>
						</span>
					</summary>
					<div class="mt-3 text-brand-body text-sm font-sans leading-relaxed border-t border-brand-hairline pt-3">
						In chess engine terms, a blunder is a severe error (typically dropping more than 2.00 pawns of value) that instantly loses material or allows checkmate. A mistake is a moderate error (losing between 0.80 and 2.00 pawns of value) that worsens your position and drops your advantage but doesn't immediately lose the game.
					</div>
				</details>

				<details class="group border border-brand-hairline rounded-md bg-brand-canvas p-4 transition-all duration-300 hover:border-brand-hairline-strong shadow-lvl2 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
					<summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-sans font-semibold text-sm text-brand-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
						<span>Can ChessNarrate explain openings in simple terms?</span>
						<span class="transition-transform duration-300 group-open:rotate-180 text-brand-mute">
							<svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2.5" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path></svg>
						</span>
					</summary>
					<div class="mt-3 text-brand-body text-sm font-sans leading-relaxed border-t border-brand-hairline pt-3">
						Yes! Our AI Narrator recognizes common chess openings (like the Ruy Lopez, Sicilian Defense, or Fried Liver Attack) and provides witty, natural language explanations. Instead of memorizing lines, you learn the core ideas behind opening setups and early development.
					</div>
				</details>

				<details class="group border border-brand-hairline rounded-md bg-brand-canvas p-4 transition-all duration-300 hover:border-brand-hairline-strong shadow-lvl2 [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
					<summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-sans font-semibold text-sm text-brand-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
						<span>How private are my uploaded chess games?</span>
						<span class="transition-transform duration-300 group-open:rotate-180 text-brand-mute">
							<svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2.5" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path></svg>
						</span>
					</summary>
					<div class="mt-3 text-brand-body text-sm font-sans leading-relaxed border-t border-brand-hairline pt-3">
						Your privacy is guaranteed. All chess evaluations are performed locally in your browser using client-side Web Workers, and your games are parsed in real time. We do not store, track, or save your chess games on any remote servers.
					</div>
				</details>
			</div>
		</section>

		<!-- JSON-LD FAQ Schema Markup -->
		<script type="application/ld+json" is:inline>
			{
				"@context": "https://schema.org",
